    pr_ref:  # 4475
    description: "## Statement (also in `fields.statement`)\n\nSymbol identity must be honest about its uncertainty across the analysis pipeline. At each stage — write, read, edge, provenance, representation — the system must either produce an unambiguous identity claim OR mark the claim as uncertain with calibrated metadata so downstream stages can filter, rank, or surface honestly. Silent collapses (single-value dicts with overwrite, hash collisions with no signal, missing producer stamps, accidental duplicate Symbol nodes for one thing) are violated forms.\n\n## The five pipeline stages\n\n| Stage | Sub-question | Open items | Closed / settled patterns |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Write** | How does an analyzer create a Symbol that uniquely identifies its referent? | `INV-fusus` (stable_id collision 91% — formula too coarse), `INV-sotiv` (stable_id missing for 7 kinds + 99.4% of files), `WI-fanun` (Symbol.fingerprint mostly null), `WI-luvig` (canonical_name docstring oversells scope) | None yet — write-side is the *unsolved* side. Mainstream analyzer per-kind identity contracts are not coherent. |\n| **Read** | When a name reference resolves to multiple candidates, how is the ambiguity expressed? | `INV-paroh` (Verilog kind-blind dict) | `INV-fosab` settled: kind-segregated registry OR calibrated-confidence multi-candidate emission (`INV-rovuj` flagship). 8 done instances under it. |\n| **Edge** | When an edge target is only partially resolved (short-name fallback), how does the edge signal that? | `INV-zuhub` (P2 pending_validation — confidence ≤ 0.5 + `disambiguation_fallback=true` flag contract) | The contract itself is the settled pattern; enforcement coverage is mid-rollout. |\n| **Provenance** | When a Symbol or Edge is created, can downstream tools trace which producer made the identity claim? | `INV-sopon` (19 of 21 linker files miss the Symbol.origin stamp), `WI-higap` (425 edges have empty origin_run_id; 1 has both origin and origin_run_id empty) | `message_queue.py:466-470` and `database_query.py:413-414` stamp correctly — positive reference. |\n| **Representation** | When the system claims a Symbol for some thing in the source, does it accidentally create two Symbols for one thing, or one Symbol with wrong kind? | `INV-hojus` (Python file double-represented as `kind=file` AND `kind=module`, 332 paths), `INV-ronuf` (WebSocket linker phantom file Symbols, 3 dupes), `INV-mopif` (HTTP linker emits `kind=function` for HTTP call sites, 18 nodes) | None settled — representation is mostly per-producer ad-hoc. The audit-cohort work under `INV-fosab` is partial precedent. |\n\n## Why this is one invariant, not five separate ones\n\nThe five stages look like independent concerns at first, but each one's correctness depends on the others:\n\n**Solutions at each stage are mirror images.** The kind-segregated registry (read side) gives lookup a list of candidates with kind metadata. A richer stable_id formula (write side) would give each candidate a unique hash so the list wouldn't be ambiguous in the first place. They solve the same conceptual problem from opposite ends of the pipeline. The 1/sqrt(N) confidence calibration in `INV-rovuj` is fundamentally a write-side honesty mechanism dressed up as a read-side fix — it says \"we can't fully identify this, here's our uncertainty\" — which is the exact same move as \"stable_id can't fully distinguish dataclasses in a module, and the system must know.\"\n\n**Provenance is structural infrastructure for the rest.** You cannot validate whether a write-side or read-side fix is working without knowing which producer made which identity claim. `INV-sopon`'s missing Symbol.origin stamps undermine every other contract on this axis. Whoever does work on this axis should land `INV-sopon` early.\n\n**Edge-side and read-side are paired.** `INV-zuhub`'s edge contract (low confidence + provenance flag) only works *if* the read side returned calibrated multi-candidate. Both halves are required for consumers to filter honestly.\n\n**Representation bugs interact with provenance.** When `INV-ronuf`'s WebSocket linker creates a phantom file-kind Symbol that duplicates the real-analyzer file Symbol, knowing both origins is what makes the duplication detectable. Fix `INV-sopon` and triage on representation bugs becomes mechanical.\n\n## Conditional decision structure (what fixes interact with what)\n\n| If you fix... | ...then this becomes easier or less urgent |\n|---|---|\n| Write-side `INV-fusus` (stable_id collisions) | Read-side kind-segregated registry becomes less load-bearing. Worth thinking whether some audit-cohort hand-rolled registries can be deprecated in favor of direct stable_id comparison. |\n| Write-side `INV-sotiv` (stable_id missing on kinds) | `INV-fosab` closure criterion gets easier — kinds currently unidentifiable at all become candidates for direct comparison. |\n| Provenance `INV-sopon` (Symbol.origin stamps) | All four other stages' fixes become *validatable* — without this, you can't tell if your write-side fix actually changed anything. **This is the foundation; should land first.** |\n| Read-side `INV-paroh` (the only open read-side instance) | Closes `INV-fosab`. If done via the shared-utility refactor route, also paves the path for retrofitting the audit-cohort siblings. |\n| Edge `INV-zuhub` (P2 pending_validation) | The producer/consumer contract becomes complete; downstream filtering (slice quality, dead-code, IO-boundaries) gets honest input. |\n| Representation `INV-hojus` / `INV-ronuf` / `INV-mopif` | Each one is a per-producer fix; they don't strongly interact with each other, but all benefit from `INV-sopon` landing first. |\n\n## Closure criterion\n\nEach of the five stages reaches `satisfied` when its open items either close OR get an explicit out-of-scope decision (the `INV-rovuj` \"satisfied flagship via alternate solution shape\" precedent). The META closes `satisfied` when all five stages are settled.\n\nThe shared `KindSegregatedRegistry` utility implied by `INV-fosab`/`WI-morud`'s escalation rule is the natural follow-on work to attach as a child of `INV-fosab` (the read-side sub-META) — not a child of this super-META — because it's a read-side implementation choice, not a pipeline-wide structural fix.\n\n## What's NOT in scope\n\n- **Edge-type semantics** (which edge types are allowed between which kinds, what their confidence floors are). That's a separate axis covered by edge-type registry items.\n- **Linker subcategory taxonomy** (Protocol / Bridge / Framework / Infrastructure per ADR-0003-ext). Orthogonal.\n- **Concept-axis declarations for multi-value fields** (ADR-0024). This META is one such axis declaration manifest, but the per-field axis declarations themselves are different artifacts."  # 4475
    fields:  # 4475
      statement: "Symbol identity must be honest about its uncertainty across the analysis pipeline. At each stage — write, read, edge, provenance, representation — the system must either produce an unambiguous identity claim OR mark the claim as uncertain with calibrated metadata so downstream stages can filter, rank, or surface honestly. Silent collapses (single-value dicts with overwrite, hash collisions with no signal, missing producer stamps, accidental duplicate Symbol nodes for one thing) are violated forms."  # 4475
      root_cause: "Each pipeline stage historically implemented its own identity logic without coordinating with the others. Write-side stable_id formulas have over-coarse inputs (INV-fusus) and narrow kind coverage (INV-sotiv); read-side lookups silently overwrite on name collisions (INV-fosab); edge-side fallback edges historically did not flag their uncertainty (INV-zuhub); provenance fields are partially populated (INV-sopon, WI-higap); representation occasionally double-counts (INV-hojus, INV-ronuf, INV-mopif). These look like independent bugs but solving one without considering its neighbors risks shipping a fix that the next stage cannot trust or build on. The cross-stage coupling makes this an architectural axis, not a bug list."  # 4475
